 gaining a column needs `drop function` first — and the new column must sit in
 the same position in the SELECT as in the RETURNS TABLE, or the mismatch
 surfaces as a type error about some unrelated column.
+
+**Two rules read the catalogue, so a new table trips them with nothing named
+by hand.** Every table in `public` must have anon's insert, update, delete,
+truncate, references and trigger revoked (probe 20260901060458 scans them
+all), and every foreign key into `courses` must be a table
+`retire_seed_course_twins` moves (the RLS section of 20261006101926 reads the
+catalogue). Revoke per table in the migration that creates it, and re-emit
+the function from `pg_get_functiondef` with the new move — never hand-copy.
+Three more that cost an hour each on the lesson-platform branch: a plpgsql
+function with `RETURNS TABLE` turns every OUT column into a variable, so an
+unqualified column name anywhere inside it (an `ON CONFLICT` target included)
+fails as ambiguous; a `count(*)` subquery beside a volatile call in the same
+`test_ok(...)` may run first as an InitPlan, so read a write in its own
+statement; and a boolean derived from a function that can return NULL
+(`room_access(s) = 'host'`) slips through `if not (...)` unless it is
+coalesced to false — which only a negative assertion catches.
+
+**A function the database calls answers before it works.** `pg_net` gives
+`invoke_edge_function`'s request about five seconds, so lesson-finalize
+returns 200 at once and does its work under `EdgeRuntime.waitUntil`; the
+database never reads the response. And a new kind of push needs a branch in
+the app's tap router (`app/(tabs)/_layout.tsx`), or its tap opens the app on
+whatever screen it was on — a quiet failure nobody reports.
